[ ] Specify or forbid a plan_path shared by several workspaces $each workspace sees the other's collections as unowned-plans-collection, quarantines them, and doctor fix may offer to delete them; every workspace also owns next and inbox collections, so names collide too. Found 2026-09-15. DEFERRED 2026-09-18, not decided: this needs investigation before a call is safe — does any real config actually share a plan_path across two clearhead workspaces today (additional_workspaces entries are separate repos with their own plans/ dirs; when would they collide)? Only once that's confirmed is it safe to decide forbid (validate at load, error out) vs support (design real cross-workspace collection ownership/namespacing) and write it into specifications/workspace.md. Out of scope for tonight's platform+specifications overnight pass; needs a clearhead-core investigation session first.$ ^2026-09-15T01:24 #01a0a42b-3be7-7691-bcbf-366e1fd87a77
[x] Drop the retired clearhead-graphd path from config.json's additional_workspaces $graphd was retired; the stale entry made any command scanning all workspace_dirs() without --charter/--file (e.g. delete action) fail with Invalid path instead of just skipping it. Found and fixed while verifying the add/delete VerbOutcome change.$ ^2026-09-17T20:54 #01a0b2a6-b3d3-714d-bb2d-347bd4df4ba5
[ ] Add an append-description verb for action resolutions $Found 2026-09-18 while running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md, whose ground rules require every action's resolution to be written into its own description before completing it. clearhead update action --description REPLACES the inline note, so all five completions that night needed a shell workaround: extract the existing description out of the .actions line by splitting on the dollar delimiters in Python, concatenate the new analysis, and pass the whole thing back. Five identical hacks, and the split is fragile for any description that itself contains a dollar sign. This charter already names the altitude -- 'Action description = the distilled per-action outcome' -- but the write verb for that altitude has no append form. Fix: an append mode (clearhead update action --append-description, or a resolve/note verb) that appends a labelled block and never asks the caller to reconstruct existing text. Acceptance: a completion can stamp its resolution in one CLI call, with a round-trip test proving the original note survives verbatim, including a note that contains a dollar sign. Touch points: clearhead-cli/src/argparser.rs and src/commands/action.rs. Cross-reference: the closed action 'Add a reopen path for completed actions' (01a030aa-2e64) named retroactive description stamping as one of its motivations, and reopen does address the frozen case. It does not address this one: with or without reopen, --description still replaces, so the caller still has to reconstruct the existing note by hand.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T10:16 #01a0b585-68c4-775a-ab77-a9ae3c17d941
[ ] Run the gate script instead of re-typing the gate $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md. The runbook lists the full pre-push gate as inline commands to run before every push; clearhead-core/.githooks/pre-push is already exactly that gate, versioned and printing its own PASS/FAIL. Duplicating the definition cost real time: the sequence was hand-assembled five times, and the hand-built version had a bug (grep exiting 1 on no matches in a pipefail shell) that killed two complete gate runs before switching to capturing test output to a file and grepping the file. Two edits. (1) platform docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md, and whatever template it is cut from, replaces the inline command list with one instruction to run the gate script, plus the single piece of shell craft that matters: send cargo test --workspace output to a file and grep the file, because the tail window truncates the failure list. (2) clearhead-core extracts scripts/gate.sh that .githooks/pre-push calls, so CI, a human, and an agent all invoke one definition instead of three. Acceptance: the runbook names one command, and that command is the same file the hook runs.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T10:16 #01a0b585-68cb-7397-b200-02ffaa6201ef
[ ] Give the run report levels distinct jobs and lead with the blocker $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md. The runbook requires the report to live in the work rather than in chat, which is right, but it produced the same facts four times: a long action description, a charter log line, a per-task checkpoint note, and a final checkpoint -- all prose summaries of one thing. Cost: writing and reading the same analysis three extra times, with drift risk between the copies. Three changes, all to the runbook and its task template. (1) Distinct roles, not copies: the action description is the full authoritative analysis; the charter log line is one line naming the decision and the commit, never a recap; the per-task checkpoint is machine-shaped (task id, commit, status); the final checkpoint is an index of those, not a narrative. (2) A stopped task's description must LEAD with the decision it needs -- a NEEDS DECISION line naming the question -- then the analysis. Two tasks stopped that night with the analysis appended after the original context, so the first thing a reader meets is the old description, not the blocker. (3) Findings outside the run's scope must still be filed as actions in the owning charter. The rule against unilaterally resolving docs-versus-log source-of-truth is right, but the run had no channel for an out-of-bounds finding, so a real one (four dead wiki-links in this charter) would have ended up only in agent-local scratchpad memory, where the project cannot see it. Acceptance: the next run's artifacts hold one full analysis, one-line logs, machine-shaped checkpoints, stopped tasks whose first line is the question, and zero findings that exist only in chat or agent memory. Note: this edits the same file as the separately-owned premise-field change, so land them together.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T10:16 #01a0b585-68d2-721c-a2da-c07fc0353c30
[ ] Attribute lens findings to the turn, not the whole file $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md. Four dead wiki-links in .clearhead/charters/support.md (lines 10, 69 and 70; present since commit 88799f81 on 2026-08-23) were reported as must-fix on roughly seven consecutive turns. The run did not introduce them: the only edit to that file was an appended log line, which put the whole file into the turn's delta, which surfaced every finding in it. That is structural rather than unlucky, because ClearHead's capture loop always appends to a charter document, so every log entry in any repository can trigger it. Three fixes in the linter, cheapest first. (1) Attribute by hunk: diff the file against HEAD for the turn and report findings only on changed lines; that alone removes the whole class. (2) If that is too coarse, add a suppression ledger that survives turns and carries provenance -- finding id, path, line, reason, who acknowledged it, when -- so the next turn reports 'four acknowledged, zero new' rather than a fresh stop. (3) Scope rules the repository deliberately does not follow to non-test code: the rust-unwrap advisory fired repeatedly on test modules where unwrap is the established convention, which trains the reader to ignore advisories. Note: the target is the agent harness linter rather than a ClearHead repository, so this is about making the tool trustworthy; it is filed here because it surfaced through dogfooding and because the capture loop is what triggers it.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T10:16 #01a0b585-68da-7037-a9d5-42f1ee1628ce
[ ] Stop dropping newlines inside an action note $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md, after passing multi-paragraph resolutions through clearhead update action --description. Newlines inside an action note are dropped with NO replacement separator, so the last word of one paragraph is joined to the first word of the next. Minimal repro: `clearhead add action probe --description "first paragraph here.<blank line>second paragraph after a blank line"` stores exactly `\[ ] probe \$first paragraph here.second paragraph after a blank line\$ ...` -- one line, and the boundary is invisible unless you look for it. Every one of the five resolutions written that night therefore reads `...don't build a second mechanism.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, ...` in the stored record. The read path has the same semantics: a hand-written note that contains a literal newline parses (no error, no lint complaint) and the model's description joins the two physical lines the same way, so a round trip through read/serialize also loses the boundary. This is not the formatter's escaping -- escape_field passes newlines through; the newline is already gone before formatting, i.e. the note field is single-line by construction at both boundaries. Why it matters now: the runbook's mandated workflow is 'write the resolution into the action's own description before completing', so notes are getting long enough to need paragraphs, and every one of them silently loses its structure. Three options, in order of ambition. (1) Minimum honest fix: normalize a newline to a single space (or reject it loudly) at both boundaries instead of deleting it, so text never silently runs together. (2) Right fix if notes are meant to carry paragraphs, which the workflow implies: allow continuation lines in the grammar so a multi-paragraph note round-trips verbatim, and teach --description to preserve what it is given. (3) If single-line notes are deliberate and permanent, say so in the DSL spec and have every writer normalize visibly. Recommendation: (1) now as a correctness fix, (2) as the real answer, since 'the distilled per-action outcome' for a multi-hour task will keep needing more than one paragraph. Touch points: the note parse/format path in clearhead-core/src/workspace/actions/parser.rs, the CLI description arguments in clearhead-cli/src/argparser.rs, and clearhead-cli/src/commands/action.rs.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T10:18 #01a0b586-aa5f-7796-aa3c-e9381eae7175
[ ] Split charter document identity from domain identity $DECIDED 2026-09-18 with the user. The file-level charter document has an OPTIONAL id, since a person may not have written it yet. The domain Charter REQUIRES one. Strictness increases at the conversion into the domain type. That conversion takes the id as an argument supplied by the shell (as Workspace from_parts already does for ephemeral_id), so clearhead-core stays pure: no clock or RNG inside parse_charter or assembly, which reverses the Uuid now_v7 added in dd20473. Resolution order: frontmatter id, then sidecar charter id, then the shell-supplied ephemeral id, never persisted by a read. Only normalize stamps a missing id, as the deliberate you-may-add-stuff pass, matching actions. Creation of a whole new document (jot on a missing file, close on an implicit charter) also writes the id, as add does for actions. No other write verb stamps as a side effect. Write verbs need no id at all: they edit document text and never round-trip through the domain Charter. Known trade-off: an id-less charter has a different identity each load, which only affects identity consumers such as query IRIs, and the charter-document-without-id lint plus doctor keep it visible. Three ordered children below. Origin: review of the overnight run, findings 1 to 3.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T11:03 #01a0b5b0-17a8-73c8-878a-cdc69737e59d
    >[ ] Make parse_charter return an optional-id document and inject the domain id $First child. Make the private CharterFrontmatter-level view a public document type with id as Option of Uuid. parse_charter returns it. Charter from-document conversion and assembly take a shell-supplied id for the missing case. Remove Uuid now_v7 from parse_charter. Keep sidecar id adoption in the resolution chain. Tests: declared id survives a retitle, undeclared gets the supplied id, parse is deterministic. Confirm the wasm dependency gate still passes.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T11:03 #01a0b5b0-17b8-77e8-82e9-971c194e64f9
    >[ ] Edit charter documents as text in update, close and jot, with the revision from the same read $Second child, and the fix for review finding 1 of the overnight run. update charter and close charter currently compute formatted from an earlier load and then capture the revision in a later read_charter_document, so a concurrent edit between them is clobbered. Derive the new text from the CharterDocument content itself so the revision and the content come from one read, as jot already does. Use surgical text edits of the frontmatter and state, not format_charter, which also silently drops defaults frontmatter. Verbs must not stamp an id as a side effect. Only creating a whole new document writes one. Add a test that a change landing between the read and the write yields a conflict for update and close, not just for a write after a read.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T11:03 <01a0b5b0-17b8-77e8-82e9-971c194e64f9 #01a0b5b0-17c4-73d7-8fa1-e9e4aebda796
    >[ ] Stamp missing charter ids in normalize and point the doctor lint at it $Third child. normalize adds a frontmatter id to any charter document that declares none, as the deliberate pass, mirroring actions. Change the charter-document-without-id finding text, which currently names clearhead update charter as the remedy, to name normalize. Test that normalize is idempotent and that doctor is clean afterwards.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:03 <01a0b5b0-17b8-77e8-82e9-971c194e64f9 #01a0b5b0-17d2-717c-938d-74ae3d848ce1
[ ] Tidy the overnight run review nits $Four small findings from the review of the overnight run. One: write_collection_displaynames uses a bare atomic_write instead of the EffectBatch seam. Two: in update_charter the displayname write runs after the md write, so a failure there returns an error for a write that already landed. Three: validate_source_path for a not-yet-existing target trusts the deepest existing ancestor, so a path such as charters/nope/../../../x.actions passes lexically and should be rejected by normalizing the path first. Four: write_charter_document has a dangling empty doc comment line.$ !4 ^2026-09-18T11:03 #01a0b5b0-17eb-7573-9921-dd40c4212d25
